    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mardavij"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mardavij.jpg" alt="Mardavij" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mardavij">Mardavij</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MardavijCoinHistoryofIran.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group; [1], CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Buyids came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylam">Daylam</a>, the mountainous region south-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a> whose hardy infantry had long resisted outside rule and whose men served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in the tenth century. Their father, Buya, was remembered as a man of modest origin, by tradition a fisherman, and his three sons, Ali, Hasan and Ahmad, rose as officers in the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardavij">Mardavij</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyarid-dynasty">Ziyarid dynasty</a>. In 934 Ali defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqut-al-hamawi">Yaqut</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> his base. After Mardavij was murdered in 935, Hasan fought for years to secure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rayy">Ray</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a>, while Ahmad advanced into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzistan</a> and, in December 945, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. The caliph al-Mustakfi gave the brothers the honorific titles by which they are known, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-dawla">Imad al-Dawla</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rukn-al-dawla">Rukn al-Dawla</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muizz-al-dawla">Mu'izz al-Dawla</a>, 'support', 'pillar' and 'strengthener' of the state; a few weeks later Mu'izz al-Dawla had him blinded and deposed and installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-muti">al-Muti</a> in his place. The Buyids thus became masters of the Abbasid capital and of most of western Iran within a dozen years. Their rise belongs to what <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-minorsky">Vladimir Minorsky</a> called the 'Iranian intermezzo', the period between Arab and Turkish domination when Iranian dynasties ruled much of the eastern caliphate.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/band-e-amir-dam"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/band-e-amir-dam.jpg" alt="Band-e Amir (dam)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/band-e-amir-dam">Band-e Amir (dam)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Band_Amir_by_Eug%C3%A8ne_Flandin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Eugène Flandin, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Buyid lands, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a>, included some of the richest agricultural and urban regions of the Islamic world, but they had suffered from decades of disorder before the brothers arrived. The Iraqi countryside in particular had declined, and the Buyids' reliance on <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a></em> grants, which assigned the tax revenue of land to soldiers in place of salaries, was blamed by contemporaries such as the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miskawayh">Miskawayh</a> for further damaging cultivation, since the holders had little interest in maintaining irrigation. Fars, the family's heartland, fared better, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a> invested in it, building the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/band-e-amir-dam">Band-e Amir</a> dam on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kor-river">Kur</a> river, which irrigated the plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marvdasht">Marvdasht</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> remained a great city and a centre of trade and scholarship, though torn by riots between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and Shia quarters and between soldiers and townspeople. Trade through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraf">Siraf</a> as its leading port, linked the Buyid lands with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The Buyids were Shia, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muizz-al-dawla">Mu'izz al-Dawla</a> introduced public mourning for Husayn on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashura">Ashura</a> and celebration of the feast of Ghadir in Baghdad in 963. Under their rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver Shia</a> scholarship flourished in Baghdad, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-shaykh-al-mufid">al-Shaykh al-Mufid</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-sharif-al-murtada">al-Sharif al-Murtada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-sharif-al-radi">al-Sharif al-Radi</a>, compiler of the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahj-al-balagha">Nahj al-Balagha</a></em>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muizz-al-dawla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muizz-al-dawla.jpg" alt="Mu'izz al-Dawla" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muizz-al-dawla">Mu'izz al-Dawla</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mu%27izz_al-Dawla_coin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group; [1], CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Buyid armies combined two very different forces: Daylamite infantry, the dynasty's own countrymen, fighting with spears, javelins and large shields, and Turkish cavalry, mostly slave soldiers or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a>, who provided the mobility the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylamites">Daylamites</a> lacked. The rivalry between these groups, over pay, status and religion, was a constant source of instability; in 974 the Turkish commander Sabuktakin rebelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muizz-al-dawla">Mu'izz al-Dawla</a>'s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izz-al-dawla">Izz al-Dawla Bakhtiyar</a>, and the mutiny helped draw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. The brothers and their heirs fought on many fronts: against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> in the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a> of Khurasan and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyarid-dynasty">Ziyarids of Gurgan</a> in the east, and rival dynasties in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, which Adud al-Dawla subdued in the late 960s. Adud al-Dawla's campaigns were the most successful: in 977–978 he defeated his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiyar-khalji">Bakhtiyar</a>, who was killed at the battle of Qasr al-Jiss in 978, and in 979 he took Mosul from the Hamdanids, while his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muayyad-al-dawla">Mu'ayyad al-Dawla</a> drove the Ziyarid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qabus">Qabus</a> out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Gurgan</a>. Adud also received the rebel Byzantine general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardas-skleros">Bardas Skleros</a>, who fled to Baghdad, and held him as a bargaining counter with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. After 983, however, Buyid armies were used mainly against other Buyids.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-dawla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imad-al-dawla.jpg" alt="Imad al-Dawla" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-dawla">Imad al-Dawla</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imad_al-Dawla_coin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group; [1], CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Buyids ruled as a family confederation rather than a single state. Each brother, and later each son, held his own territory, and the most senior was recognised as head of the family: first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-dawla">Imad al-Dawla</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> until his death in 949, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rukn-al-dawla">Rukn al-Dawla</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rayy">Ray</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> remained the formal source of legitimate authority for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslims, invested the Buyid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, and was named in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayer</a>, but he lost all real power: the emir controlled the army, the treasury and appointments, and the caliph lived on a pension. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a>, who united the Buyid lands from 977 until his death in 983, styled himself <em>shahanshah</em>, 'king of kings', reviving the old Persian royal title, and he became the first ruler to have his own name mentioned after the caliph's in the Friday prayer in Baghdad, a break with tradition. Administration followed Abbasid models, run by capable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> drawn from the secretarial class, such as Ibn al-Amid, vizier of Rukn al-Dawla, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-sahib-ibn-abbad">al-Sahib ibn Abbad</a>, vizier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muayyad-al-dawla">Mu'ayyad al-Dawla</a> and Fakhr al-Dawla at Ray. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a></em> system spread under the Buyids and later became the basis of military administration under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>. Adud al-Dawla also founded the Adudi hospital in Baghdad, opened around 982.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/book-of-fixed-stars"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/book-of-fixed-stars.jpg" alt="Book of Fixed Stars" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/book-of-fixed-stars">Book of Fixed Stars</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Unknown,_Iraq_-_The_Book_of_Fixed_Stars_-_Google_Art_Project_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Abd al-Rahman al-Sufi, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Buyid century was one of the most brilliant periods of Islamic intellectual life, sometimes called a renaissance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Buyid princes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> were generous patrons: the astronomer Abd al-Rahman al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> dedicated his <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-fixed-stars">Book of Fixed Stars</a></em> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a>, the poet al-Mutanabbi visited his court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, the grammarian Abu Ali al-Farisi wrote for him, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miskawayh">Miskawayh</a>, librarian and historian, wrote his universal history <em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajarib-al-umam">Experiences of the Nations</a></em> and works of ethics under Buyid patronage. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Ibn Sina</a> served the Buyid emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shams-al-dawla">Shams al-Dawla</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> as vizier, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-sahib-ibn-abbad">al-Sahib ibn Abbad</a> gathered scholars, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutazilism">Mu'tazili</a> theologian Abd al-Jabbar, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rayy">Ray</a>. The Buyids also shaped the history of Shia Islam: under their protection Twelver <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiism">Shiism</a> developed its theology and law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, and the public rituals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashura">Ashura</a> and Ghadir took root. Politically, they created the pattern of a secular sultan ruling in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s name, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> inherited, and they spread the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a></em> as the basis of military finance across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>. Their revival of the title <em>shahanshah</em> and of Persian royal imagery reflected a growing Iranian identity within Islamic civilisation that would later flower in Persian literature.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla.jpg" alt="Adud al-Dawla" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adud_al-Dawla_medallion.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Smithsonian, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The family structure that had allowed the Buyids to conquer so quickly became the main cause of their decline. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a>'s death in 983 his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsam-al-dawla">Samsam al-Dawla</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharaf-al-dawla">Sharaf al-Dawla</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baha-al-dawla">Baha al-Dawla</a> fought for the inheritance; Sharaf al-Dawla took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 987, and Baha al-Dawla, ruling from 989 to 1012, reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> but could not restore Adud's authority. Rival branches held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rayy">Ray</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, and local dynasties such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kakuyids">Kakuyids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasanwayhids">Kurdish Hasanwayhids</a> gained ground. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>, above all al-Qadir, reasserted their own religious authority and championed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> orthodoxy. In 1029 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of the Ghaznavid Empire</a> took Ray and deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majd-al-dawla">Majd al-Dawla</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-kalijar">Abu Kalijar</a> held Fars and Iraq until 1048, but his successors were too weak to resist the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>. In December 1055 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughril">Tughril Beg</a> of the Seljuk Empire entered Baghdad, was recognised as sultan by the caliph al-Qa'im, and imprisoned the last Buyid emir of Iraq, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-malik-al-rahim">al-Malik al-Rahim</a>. The last Buyid prince in Fars, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-mansur-fulad-sutun">Abu Mansur Fulad Sutun</a>, was overthrown and killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fadluya">Fadluya</a>, chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shabankara">Shabankara Kurds</a>, in 1062, ending the dynasty.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Buyid dynasty, Daylamite Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> who ruled western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and controlled the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> from 945 to 1055, created the model of a secular ruler beside a powerless caliph and presided over a remarkable flowering of Islamic science, philosophy and Shia scholarship.</em></p>

